+# November 2003 airline interview recovered: source, timing and recording-boundary leads
+
+October 30, 2026. TEL02/TEL04; AUD016-A1; TI-TODO01. **The nine-page November 19, 2003 American Airlines interview memorandum is now acquired from a public NARA legacy media endpoint. All nine page images were read.** This advances the original-record trail; it is an archival interview summary, not native telephone data, interview audio or a completed reconstruction.
+
+[Recovered November interview, N19](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialive/0/6096/2609600/content/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00014.pdf). Download: 1,120,488 bytes; SHA-256 `e45e7435e80b81df3f0c0376fb7be049595c1653dc6a00f1b6718c1b7dd411b3`; nine physical pages with printed footers1-9; body date November19,2003. The response was HTTP200; Content-Type was not observed and remains null. This exact representation is preserved unchanged locally.
+
+## Conflicting archive records remain separate
+
+The [NARA index](https://www.archives.gov/research/9-11/commission-memoranda.html), NAID2609600 row, describes a November19,2003 nine-page interview. The [catalog](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/2609600) still exposes `2609600_AASOC.pdf`; its viewer shows April26,2004 and three pages. The previously acquired body at that exposed URL is separately preserved: 158,153 bytes, SHA-256 `c19d9e7ae4176fb411def7259f7657d2c80bf1bdf1a803c8f4fb1b7af83bab5f`. It was not downloaded again here.
+
+The legacy URL was a constructed public-path hypothesis using an adjacent held MFR's path pattern and a filename clue, then tested against the returned bytes and all nine images. It was not presented as a link exposed by today's catalog. Finding this November body does not establish why the April association differs, when that association changed, or any substitution/removal/intent. The earlier April record and previous assessments remain unchanged. NI01 in [the ledger](OBSERVATIONS.json) preserves all three observations side by side.
+
+## What the recovered source adds
+
+**Document-assisted testimony has a shared source chain.** N19 p1 Background says participants had call transcripts and an SOCC timeline before and during the interview. Page2 explicitly assigns **7:27 a.m. Central Time** to the SOC timeline and says the interviewee did not recall the precise time. It also identifies an active incident record on an **FI form**. These are concrete source/version targets. The interview, timeline and transcript references must not be counted as independent clock measurements. NI02 pins the visible-OCR lines21-25 on p1 and2-9 on p2; OCR is a local derivative, not the PDF's native line numbering.
+
+**Time labels need an event-level join.** The recovered source and the already held [January8,2004 Halleck/Houck memorandum, H08](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialive/97/6095/2609597/content/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00011.pdf#page=3) use the following labels. H08 pages3and4 were reinspected, including its explicit memory/sequence limitations.
+
+| Source | Elapsed label | Absolute label and actual attribution |
+|---|---|---|
+| N19 p3 | 11:27, Halleck reporting back | Next paragraph separately gives7:38CT for manifest lockout; it does not explicitly timestamp the preceding report7:38. |
+| H08 p3, first paragraph | approximately11:27, Halleck communication | Staff derives about7:32CT from the tape. |
+| N19 p3, paragraph before last | 19:39, instruction to report an emergency | Nearby7:44CT concerns the ATC hijack declaration, attributed to notes reflected in the SOCC timeline; the instruction is described as prior to it. |
+| H08 p3, second paragraph | may have occurred at19:39, report of ATC information | Staff derives about7:40CT. |
+
+These are differing absolute labels and event descriptions near repeated elapsed positions, **not a verified four- or six-minute delay or proof that one clock is wrong**. The instruction, declaration and information report have not been matched to the same utterance. Do not promote the lockout time to a preceding event's timestamp. No tape was timed, clock calibrated or account selected as controlling here. Exact annotated timeline, interview notes, source recordings and same-utterance joins are required. NI03 preserves the original attribution boundaries.
+
+**The memorandum itself reports a recording boundary.** N19 p4 opening paragraph says the counter reset to zero, *apparently* because the first tape stopped or ran out, followed by an immediate replacement recording of the conversation's conclusion. It also uses approximately20:40 and later approximately22:50 for total conversation positions. This is a reported boundary and proposed cause, not an independently verified stop, uninterrupted handover, missing segment or deletion. The first/second originals, recording-system logs and common start anchor are specific TODOs. Existing held public audio derivatives and prior signal tests remain held/performed; none was played or retested in this unit. NI04.
+
+**Information control and notifications must both be preserved.** N19 pp2-4 describe internal containment, restricted manifest access and parallel operational briefings/pager notification. Page6 distinguishes no reported general intrusion-warning discussion or known ATC warning request from dispatcher-selected ACARS communications, including an all-captains message. Page8 says the airline was not on the Washington Operations Center phone net and not in continuous Herndon contact; it also reports bridge-establishment difficulty and an intermediary role for the Air Transport Association. These statements do not establish no FAA contact, a universal communications blackout or deliberate suppression. Actual instructions, FI/SOCC logs, message text/distribution/delivery and bridge/net records could strengthen or weaken those interpretations. Source accounts of confusion, ordinary nonresponses and the traditional hijack model remain contrary/alternative evidence, not adopted historical explanations. NI05.
+
+**One telephone phrase has an explicit source qualification.** N19 p2 uses “cell call” but immediately describes it as an old term for direct calls to the pilot. That is staff-reported usage, not independently verified equipment/routing, a successful call or passenger cellular-phone evidence. NI06.
+
+## Scope, preservation and next inputs
+
+All nine new pages were visually read; only the held January memorandum's pages3and4 were reinspected. Visible-image OCR of the nine new pages was generated only after image reading. No hidden PDF text, redacted identities, native call/audio content or system credentials were extracted. No independent interview, carrier reconstruction, continuity test or experimental replication occurred. Earlier April/Halleck/source reports and full original files were not changed. Current catalog-description and body observations remain unresolved side by side.
+
+The initial four-query budget was met. Two ordinary constructed-path GETs were attempted under a prospective route addendum: the first returned a 5,454-byte HTML body, not a PDF; the distinct legacy path returned the PDF above. The first HTTP200 is not documentary success or evidence of deliberate disappearance. Nine new page views plus two declared counterpart reinspections were performed. Full raw response/search/OCR/image records stay local.
+
+TI-TODO01 is partly advanced: the November scan is no longer wholly unacquired. Native notes/audio, attachments, exact timeline/transcript versions, association history and source-to-event joins remain TODO. April26/July7 source answers, Request14 delivery, carrier/operator/recipient/usage/exhibit/clock originals remain open. All18 substantive criteria across FAA, telephone, recorder, materials and steel remain open/partial; publication is not completion or proof of conspiracy. [OBSERVATIONS.json](OBSERVATIONS.json) carries the conflicts and remaining tests.
+
+The seven-file public derivative excludes PDFs, raw scans, full OCR, voices, redacted names, personnel rosters, signatures, contact/account data, private correspondence and litigation strategy. [SOURCE-PINS.json](SOURCE-PINS.json) identifies source/hash/page/line roles. [verify.py](verify.py) checks the supplied source and visible-OCR identities/selected anchors when those local inputs are provided; it does not test historical truth. [VERIFICATION.json](VERIFICATION.json) records the actual run and limits. [TEXT-CHANGES.diff](TEXT-CHANGES.diff) is the actual addition diff against absent files at this unique path, separate from image reading. [MANIFEST.json](MANIFEST.json) pins the other six files. The actual verification run passed29 checks and failed1 exact OCR-anchor check: the literal search `not in continuous contact` was absent. The source instead uses `nor were they` followed by `in continuous contact` across lines25-26 on p8. The full page was manually inspected; the failed exact search, code and result remain unchanged and public. No historical claim is made true by a green check, and this failure is not presented as a source error. Remote commit/ref and every public file body are verified separately before publication is reported complete.
